            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Ignatius of Loyola</strong><br>\nIgnatius of Loyola was born in 1491 in the castle of Loyola, in the Basque province of Guipúzcoa, Spain. As the youngest of thirteen children, he was initially drawn to the military life and the glamour of knighthood. His worldly aspirations, however, were cut short by a severe leg injury during the Battle of Pamplona against the French in 1521.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conversion and Religious Experience</strong><br>\nWhile recovering from his injury, Ignatius experienced a spiritual conversion. Reading religious texts about the life of Jesus and the saints made a deep impression on him. As a result, he decided to abandon his military career and devote his life to spiritual matters, seeking a new kind of knighthood dedicated to the service of God.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pilgrimage and Education</strong><br>\nAfter his conversion, Ignatius became a pilgrim, traveling to holy sites in Spain and then to Jerusalem in the hopes of living in the Holy Land. Upon his return, he realized the need for formal education to be effective in his religious pursuits. He studied at various universities, facing ridicule for beginning his studies late in life, and eventually earned a Master's degree in Paris at the age of 43.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
