            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Spiritual Exercises</strong><br>\nIgnatius is best known for composing the \"Spiritual Exercises,\" a guide for spiritual growth and discernment. These exercises were designed to help people understand their beliefs and find the will of God for their life decisions. They remain a fundamental part of Jesuit training and are practiced by many outside the order.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Foundation of the Society of Jesus</strong><br>\nIgnatius founded the Society of Jesus, or the Jesuits, in 1539 with a group of like-minded individuals who shared his passion for reform and education. The society was formally recognized by Pope Paul III in 1540. The Jesuits would go on to play a pivotal role in the Counter-Reformation and in educational ventures across the globe.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Jesuit Mission</strong><br>\nThe Jesuits, under Ignatius's guidance, vowed to live in poverty, chastity, and obedience. They became renowned for their strict discipline, commitment to education, and missionary work. Ignatius envisioned a nimble organization that could adapt to meet various needs, from teaching to preaching to ambassadorial roles.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
