          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Constitutions of the Society</strong><br>\nIgnatius wrote the Constitutions of the Society of Jesus, which outlined the order's organization and governance. He incorporated a central leadership with a Superior General, and stressed the importance of obedience to the Pope. This organizational structure allowed the Jesuits to manage their extensive educational and missionary activities effectively.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Last Years and Death</strong><br>\nIn his final years, Ignatius served as the first Superior General of the Jesuits and worked tirelessly to manage the rapidly expanding order. He died in Rome on July 31, 1556. His legacy included the establishment of Jesuit educational institutions that emphasized rigorous academics and spiritual formation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ignatius's Canonization and Legacy</strong><br>\nIgnatius of Loyola was canonized as a saint in 1622 by Pope Gregory XV. His feast day is celebrated on July 31. The Society of Jesus remains active today, with Jesuit educators and missionaries continuing to work worldwide in the spirit of Ignatius's dedication to service and learning.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
